  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>At the foot of the village of Le Rayol, this white-sand cove is made up of two beaches, separated by a rocky point from which a staircase serves the east beach. Crystal-clear water, the Îles d'Or in the background, picnic tables overlooking the bay (tourist office). The Domaine du Rayol, the Mediterranean gardens, is very close but does not own the beach.</p>
      <p>On the facilities side, the town's website says the back of the beach, completely redeveloped in 2019, has a children's playground, tiered seating, a beach volleyball court and fitness equipment. Since 2020, a jetty at the west end has made access easier for pleasure boats, and two underwater anti-erosion reefs are used to replenish the beach with sand. A tropical-wood terrace has been built above the beach garages, with views of the sea and the Îles d'Or. The beach is supervised in season, beach concessions offer food, sunlounger and beach gear rental, and parking is nearby (Gulf of Saint-Tropez tourist office).</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>From 1 June to 30 September, dogs on a leash are only allowed between 7pm and 8am; outside those hours, they are not allowed. This is the town hall's rule (news item of 23 June 2026 and the summer 2026 "Carnets du Rayol-Canadel-Pramousquier" bulletin), relayed by the Gulf of Saint-Tropez tourist office, and the same as on the neighbouring beaches of <a href="/en/fiche-canadel">Canadel</a> and <a href="/en/fiche-plage-pramousquier-rayol-canadel">Pramousquier</a> (Rayol-Canadel side). Owners must pick up droppings; only guide dogs for the blind and assistance dogs, identified and on a leash, remain welcome outside the hours. The town also reminds visitors that smoking on the beaches is strictly forbidden and plans to install biodegradable waste bag dispensers. For the rest of the year, neither the town nor the tourist office publishes a precise rule: ask the town hall (04 94 15 61 00).</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: in high summer, come early in the morning or in the evening (7pm-8am). The town has removed the bins from the beaches: bring bags for waste.</div>

      <h2>Getting there</h2>
      <p>On foot from the Voie Verte, the most picturesque way down goes through Square Jean Aicard and then the monumental Degrés de la Mer staircase; by bike, go down avenue Sarrazine then avenue de la Reine Jeanne. By car, from the D559, you can park in the spaces along the road, at the Patec or in front of the tourist office, or use the small car park on avenue de la Reine Jeanne. A few steps lead to the sand: an information board gives the water temperature and quality. The town specifies that its three beaches have had the highest bathing water classification since 2018.</p>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The Domaine du Rayol does not admit pets, even on a leash or in a backpack (Domaine's rules) — plan accordingly if you intend to combine both visits. <a href="/en/fiche-canadel">Canadel beach</a> is nearby, and <a href="/en/fiche-plage-pramousquier-rayol-canadel">Pramousquier beach</a> is 2 km away along the Voie Verte.</p>
